@node Core settings
@section Core settings

@vindex codex-program
@vindex codex-program-switches
@vindex codex-terminal-backend
@vindex codex-app-server-listen-url
@vindex codex-app-server-program-switches
@vindex codex-use-alt-screen
@vindex codex-disable-terminal-resize-reflow
@vindex codex-term-name
@vindex codex-startup-delay
@vindex codex-confirm-kill
@vindex codex-newline-keybinding-style
@vindex codex-enable-prompt-autosuggestions
@vindex codex-prompt-autosuggestion-placeholders
@vindex codex-prompt-autosuggestion-history-path
@vindex codex-prompt-autosuggestion-history-limit

The user option @code{codex-program} specifies the path to the Codex binary.  The default value is @code{"codex"}, which relies on the binary being in your @code{PATH}.  Change this if you have installed Codex to a non-standard location.

The user option @code{codex-program-switches} is a list of extra CLI flags to pass to terminal Codex sessions.  The default value is @code{nil}.  Use this for terminal-backend flags that you always want applied but that are not covered by the dedicated user options below.

The user option @code{codex-terminal-backend} selects the Codex backend.  The choices are @code{eat} (the default), @code{app-server}, and @code{vterm}.  The @samp{eat} and @samp{vterm} choices run the Codex terminal TUI@.  The @samp{app-server} backend starts @code{codex app-server --listen stdio://}, parses its newline-delimited JSON-RPC event stream from standard output, and renders assistant messages directly in the Emacs buffer.  Standard error is kept out of the protocol renderer and its side buffer is cleaned up with the app-server process, so CLI status logs do not appear as malformed app-server messages or leave process prompts behind when a session exits.  This avoids terminal redraw and scrollback loss for wide Markdown tables.  On the @samp{app-server} backend, @code{codex-resume} and @code{codex-fork} work natively: they list previous threads with @samp{thread/list}, prompt for one, resume or fork it through @samp{thread/resume} or @samp{thread/fork}, and replay the prior user-visible JSONL transcript when available, including the CLI-style padded user prompt rows, separators around replayed tool groups, Markdown-visible wrapping, relative file-link display, continuation indentation for replayed assistant messages, and the idle warning/composer/status block, falling back to the app-server turn history only when the transcript is unavailable.

New sessions started with @code{codex} or @code{codex-new-instance} use the global default value of @code{codex-terminal-backend}.  Existing Codex session buffers keep their actual backend in a buffer-local value so commands that act on that buffer continue to dispatch correctly.

When an @samp{app-server} session connects, codex.el renders the same startup banner shape as the terminal TUI: Codex version, model, reasoning effort, service tier, working directory, and the standard keymap tip.  App-server notifications are rendered only when they belong to the buffer's current thread, so sub-agent or side-thread events do not leak command output into the parent thread's buffer.

The @samp{app-server} buffer presents an editable input region after a prompt at the end of the buffer.  Type a message there and press the send key to submit it; conversation output renders above the prompt while the rendered history stays read-only and fully scrollable.  The send and newline keys follow @code{codex-newline-keybinding-style}, so a multi-line prompt is composed with the newline key and submitted with the send key.  As in the Codex CLI, sending while a turn is active steers the current turn.  Pressing @samp{TAB} accepts the visible idle suggestion, completes a slash command or @samp{$} skill reference when idle, and queues the input to run as the next turn while a turn is in progress.  Queued inputs render in a live @code{• Queued follow-up inputs} block (one @code{↳} line each), exactly as the CLI shows them; @samp{M-<up>} pulls the last queued input back into the composer to edit it.  While a turn is active, a @code{• Working (Ns • esc to interrupt)} line shows above the prompt, and @samp{ESC} (or @samp{C-g}) interrupts the turn like the CLI's Esc.  Several composer affordances match the CLI: a leading @samp{!} runs the rest of the line as a shell command in the thread, the idle suggestion appears after the cursor through Emacs completion preview with Codex's autosuggestion styling when that native preview facility is available and the composer is empty, typing @samp{@@} completes a project file path into the message, @samp{M-p} and @samp{M-n} cycle through previously sent prompts (with @samp{C-c C-r} searching them by completion), @samp{C-c C-e} opens a separate buffer to compose a long prompt that is sent back with @samp{C-c C-c}, and @samp{C-c C-<up>} / @samp{C-c C-<down>} raise and lower the reasoning effort for upcoming turns (matching the CLI's reasoning up/down).  The composer remains the last buffer text so moving to the end of the buffer lands in editable input.  The user option @code{codex-app-server-prompt-string} sets the prompt shown before the input region; the default is @code{"\n› "}.

Skill completion searches the configured @code{codex-app-server-skill-directories} and project-local @samp{.claude/skills}, @samp{.claude/programmatic-skills}, @samp{.codex/skills}, and @samp{.codex/programmatic-skills} directories in the Codex session directory and its ancestors.

The @samp{app-server} backend renders conversation items the way the Codex CLI does, using the same item prefixes: user messages lead with @code{›} and every agent-side item (assistant messages, reasoning, commands, file changes, plans) leads with @code{•}, with continuation lines indented to align under the bullet and one blank line between items.  Command executions are collapsed exactly like the CLI: a @code{• Ran COMMAND} header (or @code{✗ Ran COMMAND (exit N)} on failure), then the first @code{codex-app-server-max-command-output-lines} output lines (default 3, matching the CLI), a @code{… +N lines} marker, and the final output line, indented under a @code{└} connector.  Commands with no output render just the command header.  This avoids dumping full command output (which can be megabytes) into the buffer; the full output is available with @code{codex-app-server-expand-output} (@samp{C-c C-o}).  Commands that only read files are grouped into a @code{• Explored} block with a @code{└ Read FILE, FILE} line and no output dump, exactly as the CLI collapses pure file reads.  Reasoning summaries render as dimmed text; file changes render as a @code{• Edited FILE (+N -M)} header followed by the diff numbered and marked like the CLI (~    4 -four~ / ~    4 +FOUR~), with added and removed line faces; MCP tool calls render as @code{• Called SERVER.TOOL(ARGS)} with the folded text result under @code{└}, like the CLI; web searches render as @code{• Searched the web for QUERY} like the CLI; and the turn plan renders as a @code{• Updated Plan} tree with @code{✔} for completed and @code{□} for pending steps, updating in place as steps complete.  Turn errors render as a status line showing the server's error message, including the message nested under a turn error, so they are not silently dropped.

When Codex requests approval, codex.el prompts with the same choices the CLI offers for that request.  For a command, the choices come from the request's @code{availableDecisions} -- approve once, approve and stop asking for this command (the execpolicy amendment), or decline -- so the decision sent back is always one the server accepts.  For a file change, the choices are apply once, apply for the rest of the session, decline, or cancel the turn.

While a turn is running, codex.el shows a @samp{• Working (Ns • esc to interrupt)} line above the prompt, matching the CLI status line, plus a mode-line indicator that also reports token usage.  Both clear when the turn completes.

On the @samp{app-server} backend, slash commands typed into the input are handled by the frontend instead of being sent to the model.  Protocol-backed commands invoke the same Codex app-server methods used by native clients: @code{/skills} lists skills and can insert or enable/disable one; @code{/plugins} lists, installs, enables/disables, or uninstalls plugins; @code{/plugin-mention} inserts a plugin as a @code{$} mention, and is also bound to @code{$} in the composer, mirroring the CLI, whose @code{$} menu lists installed and enabled plugins; @code{/hooks} lists hooks and can enable, disable, or trust one; @code{/experimental} lists and persistently toggles features; @code{/mcp}, @code{/ps}, @code{/debug-config}, and @code{/usage} read their corresponding Codex inventories; @code{/permissions} lists and applies a Codex permission profile; @code{/feedback} uploads feedback; @code{/logout} logs out after confirmation; @code{/fast} persistently updates the service tier and applies it to the current thread; @code{/stop} lists and terminates background terminals; and @code{/delete} deletes the current thread after confirmation.  Thread and model commands such as @code{/compact}, @code{/model}, @code{/goal}, @code{/goal-clear}, @code{/rename}, @code{/archive}, @code{/unarchive}, @code{/memories}, and @code{/personality} likewise use their matching app-server requests.  Commands whose native behavior belongs specifically to the terminal TUI, or for which the app-server exposes no equivalent operation, are not advertised by app-server completion and are reported as unsupported rather than being replaced by an Emacs-specific action.  Frontend actions with the same observable result remain available: @code{/clear} and @code{/new} start a new conversation, @code{/diff} shows the agent's own changes for the thread rather than the working tree, matching the CLI, which reports no changes when the only edits present are the user's own; @code{/copy} copies the last reply, @code{/resume} and @code{/fork} use the native thread requests, @code{/mention} attaches a file, @code{/raw} toggles rendered versus raw output, @code{/init} submits the same task prompt, and @code{/review} starts a native review through @code{review/start}, offering the CLI's four presets: a base branch, uncommitted changes, a commit, or custom instructions.  Lifecycle hook events (@code{hook/started}, @code{hook/completed}) render as dimmed status lines when @code{codex-app-server-show-hooks} is enabled, and primary rate-limit usage is recorded and shown by @code{/status}.  The app-server backend reads account rate limits during startup; when weekly usage is high, the CLI-style weekly-limit warning renders above the idle composer.  Other warnings, deprecation and guardian notices, conversation compaction, model reroutes, MCP server startup progress and failures, and thread name and goal updates also render as status lines.

The experimental realtime channel is handled too: @code{codex-app-server-realtime-start}, @code{codex-app-server-realtime-stop}, and @code{codex-app-server-realtime-send-text} control a text-output realtime session, and realtime lifecycle and transcript events render in the buffer (audio streaming and SDP signaling are accepted but not rendered, since they have no text representation).

Because command output is folded, @code{codex-app-server-expand-output} (bound to @code{C-c C-o}) shows the full, unfolded output of the command block at point in a dedicated buffer, mirroring the CLI transcript view.

Images can be attached to a turn on the @samp{app-server} backend: @code{codex-default-images} are attached to the first prompt, @code{codex-app-server-attach-image} attaches an image file to the next turn, and @code{codex-app-server-paste-image} (bound to @code{C-v}, matching the Codex CLI's Ctrl+V) attaches an image directly from the system clipboard and inserts an @code{[Image #N]} placeholder into the composer at point, exactly as the CLI does.  Attached images are sent as @samp{localImage} input items.

Completed assistant messages in @samp{app-server} buffers are rendered as Markdown the way the Codex CLI renders them.  When @samp{markdown-mode} is available, codex.el fontifies each message through @samp{gfm-mode} and, like the CLI, hides only the inline emphasis markers (so @samp{**bold**} and @samp{`code`} show as styled text without the delimiters) while keeping block markers such as heading @samp{#} and list @samp{-} visible and syntax-coloured.  The user option @code{codex-app-server-render-markdown} enables this rendering; the default is @code{t}.  When it is nil, or when @samp{markdown-mode} is not installed, a lightweight built-in highlighter is used instead.

The user option @code{codex-app-server-listen-url} controls the transport passed to @code{codex app-server --listen}.  The default is @code{"stdio://"}, which keeps the server private to the Emacs process.

The user option @code{codex-app-server-program-switches} is a list of extra CLI flags to pass to @code{codex app-server}.  The default value is @code{nil}.

The user option @code{codex-use-alt-screen} controls whether terminal backends run Codex in its default alternate-screen TUI mode.  The default is @code{nil}, so codex.el starts terminal sessions with @code{--no-alt-screen} for inline scrollback mode.  This avoids recurring stale-screen failures seen when Codex's alternate-screen TUI and Emacs terminal buffers lose synchronization after interrupts, prompt editing, or heavy redraws.  Set it to non-nil only if you explicitly want Codex's alt-screen TUI@.

The user option @code{codex-disable-terminal-resize-reflow} controls whether codex.el passes @code{--disable terminal_resize_reflow} to new terminal-backend Codex sessions.  The default is @code{t} because Codex's experimental terminal reflow rebuilds scrollback after width changes, while Emacs terminal buffers use the buffer itself as retained session history.  Disable this option only if you prefer Codex's own terminal reflow behavior.

The user option @code{codex-term-name} overrides the terminal type reported to Codex by terminal backends.  The default is @code{nil}, meaning codex.el chooses a backend-appropriate @code{TERM} value.  For @samp{eat}, Codex buffers use Eat's bundled @samp{eat-*} terminfo even if the global @samp{eat-term-name} has been customized; for @samp{vterm}, Codex buffers keep vterm's own default.  Set this to a string such as @code{"xterm-256color"} only when your terminal environment requires a specific @code{TERM} value.  The value is applied when the terminal subprocess starts, so changes affect new Codex buffers rather than already-running sessions.

In @samp{eat} buffers, codex.el disables Eat shell integration for the Codex subprocess and ignores Eat's private UI-command channel in the outer terminal, including in existing Codex buffers after codex.el is reloaded.  Codex is itself a TUI that captures and re-renders tool output; if nested shell commands emit Eat-private OSC sequences, those sequences can leak into captured tool output and corrupt the outer terminal display.

In @samp{eat} Codex buffers, codex.el also coalesces terminal output chunks that end in the middle of a CSI escape sequence.  This protects Codex sessions from a known failure mode where the CLI emits a cursor-shape sequence such as @samp{ESC [ 0 SPC q} across two process-output chunks and Eat tries to parse the incomplete first half as a complete sequence.  Codex additionally strips aborted CSI fragments --- a CSI sequence terminated by ESC instead of a final byte --- because Eat misroutes the abort byte as part of the CSI function, desynchronising its cursor tracking and tripping an assertion in @samp{eat--t-cur-left} on the next cursor move.  If Eat still rejects an output chunk, codex.el logs the parser error, retries that chunk as plain readable text with terminal controls removed, and then keeps processing later queued output instead of letting one malformed chunk abort the whole burst.  Codex's post-output maintenance hooks are also isolated from Eat's output queue, so display remapping or prompt autosuggestion errors are logged instead of repeatedly aborting @samp{eat--process-output-queue}.

The user option @code{codex-startup-delay} is a number of seconds to wait after starting the Codex process before displaying the buffer.  The default is @code{0.1}.  Increasing this value can help fix terminal layout issues that occur when the buffer is displayed before Codex has fully initialized.

The user option @code{codex-confirm-kill} controls whether @code{codex-kill} and @code{codex-kill-all} ask for confirmation before killing instances.  The default is @code{t}.  Set it to @code{nil} if you prefer to kill instances without a prompt.

The user option @code{codex-newline-keybinding-style} controls how the return key behaves in Codex buffers.  The default, @code{newline-on-shift-return}, makes @code{S-RET} insert a line break and plain @code{RET} submit the prompt, mirroring the behavior of @samp{claude-code.el}.  The other choices are @code{newline-on-alt-return} (@code{M-RET} for newline, @code{RET} to submit), @code{shift-return-to-send} (@code{RET} for newline, @code{S-RET} to submit), and @code{super-return-to-send} (@code{RET} for newline, @code{s-RET} — the COMMAND key on macOS — to submit).  The line-break action is delivered to Codex as @code{Ctrl+J} (byte @code{0x0A}), which the Codex CLI binds to its @code{insert_newline} editor action by default; submission is delivered as a plain @code{Return}.

In @samp{eat} buffers, plain Return is dispatched as the same @samp{RET} character event
used by normal terminal input; Escape also goes through Eat's key-event input
path rather than raw string insertion.  This keeps programmatic TUI actions
aligned with real keypresses.

The user option @code{codex-enable-prompt-autosuggestions} controls whether @samp{codex.el} styles prompt autosuggestions in @samp{eat} buffers.  The default is @code{t}.  Codex renders placeholder and suggestion text after the terminal cursor; when that text arrives in Emacs without the CLI's dim styling, @samp{codex.el} recognizes known suggestions and applies @code{codex-prompt-autosuggestion-face}, which inherits @code{shadow} without forcing italic text.  While an autosuggestion is visible, @samp{codex.el} keeps point at the prompt cursor rather than the footer line that Codex renders below the prompt.  The options @code{codex-prompt-autosuggestion-placeholders}, @code{codex-prompt-autosuggestion-history-path}, and @code{codex-prompt-autosuggestion-history-limit} control which built-in placeholder strings and recent history entries are recognized as autosuggestions.  In @samp{eat} Codex buffers, @samp{TAB} first accepts the visible autosuggestion when there is one; otherwise it is sent through to the Codex TUI as a normal tab.

@node Sandbox and approval
@section Sandbox and approval

@vindex codex-sandbox-mode
@vindex codex-approval-policy
@vindex codex-full-auto

The user option @code{codex-sandbox-mode} controls the sandboxing level for Codex.  When @code{nil} (the default), the CLI's own default is used.  The other choices are @code{read-only}, @code{workspace-write}, and @code{danger-full-access}, which correspond to the CLI's @code{--sandbox} flag values.  This option is ignored when @code{codex-full-auto} is non-nil.

The user option @code{codex-approval-policy} sets the approval policy for tool use.  When @code{nil} (the default), the CLI default applies.  The choices are @code{untrusted}, @code{on-request}, and @code{never}, corresponding to the @code{--ask-for-approval} flag.  This option is also ignored when @code{codex-full-auto} is non-nil.

The user option @code{codex-full-auto} enables fully autonomous mode by passing @code{--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox} to Codex.  The default is @code{nil}.  When non-nil, this overrides both @code{codex-sandbox-mode} and @code{codex-approval-policy}.  Use with caution.

@node Model and profile
@section Model and profile

@vindex codex-model
@vindex codex-profile
@vindex codex-reasoning-effort

The user option @code{codex-model} specifies a model override (e.g., @code{"gpt-5.4"}).  The default is @code{nil}, which uses the CLI's default model.

The user option @code{codex-profile} names a configuration profile.  The default is @code{nil}, meaning the CLI default profile is used.  Profiles allow you to maintain separate sets of configuration for different workflows.

The user option @code{codex-reasoning-effort} overrides the reasoning effort level.  The default is @code{nil}.  When set to a string value, it is passed via @code{-c} as the @code{model_reasoning_effort} configuration value.

@node Hooks integration
@section Hooks integration

@vindex codex-enable-hooks
@vindex codex-hooks-config-path
@vindex codex-hooks-json-path
@vindex codex-emacsclient-program

The user option @code{codex-enable-hooks} controls whether @samp{codex.el} automatically configures the Codex CLI hooks system.  The default is @code{t}.  When enabled, activating @code{codex-mode} ensures that @code{config.toml} has @code{hooks = true} under @code{[features]} and that @code{hooks.json} contains entries for all supported hook types.  The installed hook wrapper forwards only sessions that @samp{codex.el} launched with @code{CODEX_BUFFER_NAME} set; other Codex sessions consume the hook input and continue without contacting Emacs.

The user option @code{codex-hooks-config-path} is the path to the Codex @code{config.toml} file.  The default is @code{"~/.codex/config.toml"}.

The user option @code{codex-hooks-json-path} is the path to the Codex @code{hooks.json} file.  The default is @code{"~/.codex/hooks.json"}.

The user option @code{codex-emacsclient-program} specifies the @samp{emacsclient} executable used by the hook wrapper.  The default is @code{nil}, meaning @samp{codex.el} records the first @samp{emacsclient} found in @code{PATH} when hooks are configured.

@node Background and contrast remapping
@section Background and contrast remapping

@vindex codex-remap-light-backgrounds
@vindex codex-card-background
@vindex codex-background-contrast-threshold
@vindex codex-minimum-contrast-ratio

The Codex CLI has its own theme system, and the colors it emits do not always match the Emacs theme.  Mismatches make terminal text unreadable in two directions: the CLI may paint light backgrounds on a dark Emacs theme (bright rectangles containing dark text), or dark backgrounds on a light Emacs theme (dark rectangles containing light text).  A third kind of mismatch is low-contrast foreground colors that wash out against the effective background (e.g. bright cyan on a light default background, or dark red on a dark diff background).  The remapping system runs after each batch of terminal output and addresses all three.

The user option @code{codex-remap-light-backgrounds} is the master switch.  The default is @code{t}.  When non-nil, background colors whose WCAG contrast against the Emacs default background exceeds @code{codex-background-contrast-threshold} are stripped (or replaced), and --- when @code{codex-minimum-contrast-ratio} is also non-nil --- low-contrast foregrounds are stripped as well.

The user option @code{codex-card-background} specifies the replacement color for clashing backgrounds.  When @code{nil} (the default), clashing backgrounds are stripped entirely so the Emacs buffer background shows through.  Set this to a color string (e.g. @code{"#1a1b2e"}) to use a fixed replacement color instead.

The user option @code{codex-background-contrast-threshold} is a WCAG contrast ratio above which a CLI background is considered to clash with the Emacs theme.  The default is @code{1.0}, which strips any explicit background that is not identical to the Emacs default background --- the cleanest result for users who want the Emacs theme to fully own the rendering.  Codex's diff foregrounds (colored @code{+} / @code{-} glyphs) still distinguish added and removed lines after the bg strip.  Raise this to @code{3.0} (the WCAG AA threshold for large text) to preserve subtle low-contrast tints (e.g. light-green diff backgrounds on a light theme at contrast @code{1.04:1}) and only strip clashing rectangles.  Because the comparison is against the Emacs default background, the same threshold handles mismatches in either direction.

The user option @code{codex-minimum-contrast-ratio} is a WCAG contrast ratio below which the explicit foreground is stripped so the Emacs theme's default foreground takes over.  The default is @code{3.0}.  Set this to @code{nil} to disable contrast-based foreground remapping while keeping background remapping.  Stripping the foreground rather than the background preserves the semantic tint of diff-added and diff-removed backgrounds while ensuring the text itself remains readable.

Because the remapping pass runs after every batch of terminal output, it only inspects the current eat display plus newly appended output since the previous pass, not the full retained scrollback.  Perceived luminance results are also memoized in @code{codex--color-luminance-cache} — a process-wide hash table keyed by color string.  The cache is small (one entry per distinct color) and is preserved across reloads of the package, so repeated contrast comparisons reduce to O(1) hash lookups instead of repeated @code{color-name-to-rgb} calls.

@menu
* Why is this a post-hoc remap instead of a clean fix?::
@end menu

@node Why is this a post-hoc remap instead of a clean fix?
@subsection Why is this a post-hoc remap instead of a clean fix?

This feature exists because the Codex CLI emits 24-bit RGB escape codes for card backgrounds and some foregrounds.  Those land as literal @code{:foreground} / @code{:background} text properties in the eat buffer, bypassing the Emacs face system entirely — there is no indirection point where the Emacs theme gets to participate.

The clean alternative would be to downgrade Codex to 256-color (which eat routes through @code{eat-term-color-*} faces that the Emacs theme already controls).  That was attempted by setting @code{COLORTERM=} (empty) in the Codex subprocess environment.  It did not work: Codex's Rust UI code calls @code{Color::Rgb(...)} directly in several places (chat composer, diff blocks), bypassing its own @code{stdout_color_level()} detection.  No env var or config key currently turns those call sites off, and @code{NO_COLOR=1} is too aggressive (it disables all color).

This machinery should be revisited and deleted if Codex stops hardcoding @code{Color::Rgb} in its UI code, grows a config option to use the terminal palette, or starts honoring @code{COLORTERM} consistently.  To verify: start a fresh Codex session and check whether the buffer contains literal @code{#RRGGBB} hex colors in its face text properties — if all colors route through @code{eat-term-color-*} faces, this whole section can go.

@node Notifications
@section Notifications

@vindex codex-enable-notifications
@vindex codex-notification-function

The user option @code{codex-enable-notifications} controls whether notifications are shown when Codex finishes a task and is waiting for input.  The default is @code{t}.  Notifications are triggered by bell characters from the terminal or by @code{Stop} hook events.

The user option @code{codex-notification-function} specifies the function called for notifications.  The function receives two arguments: a title string and a message string.  The default is @code{codex-default-notification}, which displays a message in the echo area and pulses the mode line (@ref{Notification functions}).

@node Window management
@section Window management

@vindex codex-no-delete-other-windows
@vindex codex-toggle-auto-select
@vindex codex-optimize-window-resize
@vindex codex-display-window-fn

The user option @code{codex-no-delete-other-windows} prevents Codex windows from being deleted by @code{delete-other-windows}.  The default is @code{nil}.  Set this to @code{t} if you want the Codex window to persist when you use @samp{C-x 1} or similar commands.

The user option @code{codex-toggle-auto-select} controls whether @code{codex-toggle} automatically selects the Codex window after opening it.  The default is @code{nil}, meaning the window is displayed but focus stays in the current window.

The user option @code{codex-optimize-window-resize} controls whether terminal reflows are suppressed when the window size has not changed.  The default is @code{t}.  This avoids redundant redraws while still keeping the terminal dimensions synchronized after width or height changes.

The user option @code{codex-display-window-fn} specifies the function used to display the Codex window.  It must accept a buffer argument.  The default is @code{codex-display-buffer-same-window}, which displays the Codex buffer in the current window.  An alternative @code{codex-display-buffer-below} is also provided, which displays it below the currently selected window.

@node Images
@section Images

@vindex codex-default-images

The user option @code{codex-default-images} is a list of image file paths to attach at startup via the @code{--image} flag.  The default is @code{nil}.  Use this to always include certain screenshots or diagrams as context.

@node Emacs hooks
@section Emacs hooks

@vindex codex-start-hook
@vindex codex-command-submitted-hook
@vindex codex-process-environment-functions
@vindex codex-event-hook
@vindex codex-transcript-catch-up-on-stop
@vindex codex-transcript-sessions-directory

The hook @code{codex-start-hook} runs after a Codex instance starts.  Use it to perform additional setup in the Codex buffer, such as enabling minor modes or customizing keybindings.

The abnormal hook @code{codex-command-submitted-hook} runs before input is submitted to a Codex session; each function receives the session buffer, with that buffer current, and must be idempotent because a single submission may run the hook more than once.

The abnormal hook @code{codex-process-environment-functions} lets you inject environment variables into the Codex process.  Each function receives two arguments: the Codex buffer name and the project directory.  Each function should return a list of strings in @code{"VAR=VALUE"} format.

The hook @code{codex-event-hook} runs when the Codex CLI triggers lifecycle events through the hooks system.  This is an abnormal hook: functions are called with a single plist argument containing @code{:type}, @code{:buffer-name}, @code{:json-data}, and @code{:args}, and dispatch stops at the first non-nil return value.  That value is returned to the Codex CLI hook process, which lets permission and tool hooks approve, deny, or block.

The user option @code{codex-transcript-catch-up-on-stop} controls whether Stop hooks repair stale terminal output from Codex JSONL transcripts.  This catch-up repair applies only to the @samp{eat} and @samp{vterm} terminal backends.  The @samp{app-server} backend uses JSONL transcripts for resumed history replay when available, but live app-server turns still render from structured protocol events and do not use Stop-hook catch-up.  The default is @code{nil} because Stop hooks can run before the JSONL transcript has recorded the just-finished turn, and an automatic repair may otherwise append an older assistant message into the live buffer.  When enabled, @code{codex.el} tracks the session id from hook JSON, finds the transcript under @code{codex-transcript-sessions-directory}, and inserts only the missing suffix when the terminal buffer already shows the beginning of the answer.  Repair text is placed at the visible truncation point only when transcript lines match the buffer in order, or before the active prompt, so unrelated repeated lines earlier in scrollback do not suppress repair.  Prefer calling @code{codex-refresh-from-transcript} explicitly to repair a known stale buffer after the transcript has settled.

@node Eat terminal settings
@section Eat terminal settings

@vindex codex-eat-read-only-mode-cursor-type
@vindex codex-eat-disable-cursor-blink
@vindex codex-eat-scrollback-size
@vindex codex-eat-preserve-scrollback

The user option @code{codex-eat-read-only-mode-cursor-type} specifies the cursor appearance when the eat terminal is in read-only mode.  The value is a list of the form @code{(CURSOR-ON BLINKING-FREQUENCY CURSOR-OFF)}.  The default is @code{(box nil nil)}, which shows a non-blinking filled box cursor.

The user option @code{codex-eat-disable-cursor-blink} controls whether Codex @samp{eat} buffers map blinking terminal cursor states to non-blinking equivalents.  The default is @code{t}.  This keeps the terminal cursor visible while avoiding Eat's graphical cursor blink timer, which redraws the whole frame and can flicker on macOS@.  Set this to @code{nil} if you want Codex to honor blinking cursor states exactly as emitted by the CLI@.

The user option @code{codex-eat-scrollback-size} controls how much scrollback eat keeps for Codex buffers.  The default is @code{nil}, meaning unlimited scrollback.  This avoids losing earlier Codex output in long sessions; set it to a number of characters if you want a bounded buffer.

The user option @code{codex-eat-preserve-scrollback} controls whether Codex @samp{eat} buffers strip CSI erase-display commands that can delete retained history before Eat processes output.  The default is @code{t}.  Codex runs with @code{--no-alt-screen} by default so the Emacs buffer can serve as session history, but TUI redraws can still emit erase-above, erase-all, or erase-scrollback sequences that make Eat delete retained buffer text.  Ordinary erase-below redraw commands still pass through so prompt menus and status areas can clear stale text.

The package also defines faces for the eat terminal that inherit from the corresponding @samp{eat} faces: @code{codex-eat-prompt-annotation-running-face}, @code{codex-eat-prompt-annotation-success-face}, @code{codex-eat-prompt-annotation-failure-face}, @code{codex-eat-term-bold-face}, @code{codex-eat-term-faint-face}, @code{codex-eat-term-italic-face}, @code{codex-eat-term-slow-blink-face}, @code{codex-eat-term-fast-blink-face}, and font faces @code{codex-eat-term-font-0-face} through @code{codex-eat-term-font-9-face}.  Customize these faces if you want the Codex terminal to look different from other eat buffers.

@node Vterm terminal settings
@section Vterm terminal settings

@vindex codex-vterm-buffer-multiline-output
@vindex codex-vterm-multiline-delay
@vindex codex-vterm-max-scrollback

The user option @code{codex-vterm-buffer-multiline-output} controls whether multi-line terminal output is buffered to prevent flickering.  The default is @code{t}.  When enabled, rapid successive output chunks containing cursor movement sequences are accumulated and flushed as a single update.

The user option @code{codex-vterm-multiline-delay} sets the delay (in seconds) before processing buffered vterm output.  The default is @code{0.01}.  Increase this if you still see flickering with the buffering enabled.

The user option @code{codex-vterm-max-scrollback} controls how many scrollback lines vterm keeps for Codex buffers.  The default is @code{100000}, which is vterm's built-in maximum unless its native module is recompiled with a larger limit.

